 ### [Ray Marching and Discretization](2788-ray-marching-and-discretization.md)
-An iterative technique that approximates the volume rendering integral by casting rays and accumulating optical properties at discrete sample points. Unlike fixed-step traversal, iterative variants may use variable step sizes guided by distance estimators, enabling efficient handling of sparse or implicit data. It processes pixels in a fragment shader, ensuring view-independent quality.
+An iterative direct volume rendering technique that approximates the volume rendering integral by casting rays from the camera through a 3D volume and accumulating optical properties at discrete sample points. It solves the problem of visualizing semi-transparent scalar fields without extracting explicit geometric surfaces by mapping data values to color and opacity via a transfer function and integrating the emission-absorption model along the viewing ray. Unlike fixed-step traversal methods, iterative variants advance rays using variable step sizes guided by distance estimators, enabling efficient handling of sparse, implicit, or procedural data. It processes the image pixel-by-pixel in a fragment shader, ensuring view-independent quality and supporting early ray termination.
 
 ### [Image-order Rendering Loop](2938-image-order-rendering-loop.md)
-The primary control structure for direct volume ray casting that iterates over output pixels to generate camera rays and invoke a raymarcher. Unlike object-order methods, it offers correct perspective projection and full 32-bit compositing precision without intermediate buffers.
+The fundamental control structure for direct volume ray casting, iterating over each pixel of the output image to generate a ray, traverse the volume, and composite the result. It solves the problem of mapping 3D volumetric data to a 2D image plane by decoupling the sampling process from the data structure, allowing for adaptive sampling and correct perspective projection in a single pass. Unlike object-order methods (rasterization), which process data primitives first, this approach is described as "CPU-like" but offers flexibility for advanced illumination and 32-bit compositing precision. It builds on the shared principles of [Image-order rendering](16-image-order-rendering.md) and [Ray Traversal and Integration Schemes](8341-ray-traversal-and-integration-schemes.md).
 
